<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilica of San Zeno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as San Zeno Maggiore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basilica of San Zeno in Verona is a Romanesque church built mainly in the 12th century over the tomb of Verona's patron saint, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Zeno. Its façade has a large rose window and bronze doors with biblical reliefs, and its high altar holds a triptych by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-mantegna">Andrea Mantegna</a>. It is a masterpiece of northern Italian Romanesque.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-zeno.jpg" alt="Basilica of San Zeno" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_di_san_zeno_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
